1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,767
How do language models see text?

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:04,195
Not as letters, and not as words.

3
00:00:04,235 --> 00:00:08,437
They see tokens: small pieces of
text, each one mapped to a number.

4
00:00:08,767 --> 00:00:13,652
This video walks through the tokenization
primer: how tokens are created,

5
00:00:13,692 --> 00:00:16,139
why they sometimes behave
in surprising ways,

6
00:00:16,179 --> 00:00:19,069
and which tools you can
use to work with them.

7
00:00:20,267 --> 00:00:25,622
Many of the strange behaviours of large
language models start with tokenization.

8
00:00:25,662 --> 00:00:28,487
Here are four examples
that we will come back to.

9
00:00:28,833 --> 00:00:29,933
Letter counting.

10
00:00:29,973 --> 00:00:32,538
A token can contain several letters,

11
00:00:32,578 --> 00:00:36,063
so character-level tasks are
less direct for the model.

12
00:00:36,103 --> 00:00:42,454
GPT-4, for example, has incorrectly reported
two r's in strawberry instead of three.

13
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:43,805
Arithmetic.

14
00:00:43,845 --> 00:00:49,690
A number such as 12345 may
be split into 123 and 45,

15
00:00:49,730 --> 00:00:51,696
rather than into individual digits.

16
00:00:52,033 --> 00:00:53,068
Languages.

17
00:00:53,108 --> 00:00:56,823
Equivalent messages can require
different numbers of tokens,

18
00:00:56,863 --> 00:01:00,050
and that affects how much of
the context window they use.

19
00:01:00,367 --> 00:01:01,352
And programming.

20
00:01:01,392 --> 00:01:06,580
The way whitespace is tokenized affects how
much code fits into the context window.

21
00:01:07,767 --> 00:01:12,334
Where does the tokenizer fit? It sits
between raw text and the language model.

22
00:01:12,667 --> 00:01:16,807
Encoding turns a text string
into a sequence of token IDs.

23
00:01:16,847 --> 00:01:20,029
That sequence is what the
model actually receives.

24
00:01:20,367 --> 00:01:25,337
The model answers with token IDs as well,
and decoding turns them back into text.

25
00:01:25,667 --> 00:01:29,832
The tokenizer is trained separately,
on its own set of documents.

26
00:01:29,872 --> 00:01:35,167
Training uses the byte pair encoding
algorithm, and it is a one-time procedure.

27
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:38,585
The result is a vocabulary
and a list of merges.

28
00:01:38,625 --> 00:01:42,468
Once we have those two, we
can both encode and decode.

29
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:45,880
The language model has
its own training dataset,

30
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:50,115
which is much larger than the one
typically used to train the tokenizer.

31
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:54,916
Here is a brief history of byte
pair encoding, in three steps.

32
00:01:55,233 --> 00:02:00,033
In 1994, Philip Gage introduced
it as a compression algorithm.

33
00:02:00,073 --> 00:02:06,233
It repeatedly replaces the most
frequent pair of adjacent bytes
with a single unused byte,

34
00:02:06,273 --> 00:02:08,832
which gives a more compact representation.

35
00:02:09,167 --> 00:02:15,717
In 2016, Sennrich, Haddow and Birch
adapted BPE for subword tokenization.

36
00:02:15,757 --> 00:02:21,022
Their version merges characters and
character sequences into reusable pieces.

37
00:02:21,062 --> 00:02:26,633
This helps neural machine translation
models represent rare and unseen words.

38
00:02:26,967 --> 00:02:32,977
And in 2019, GPT-2 used byte-level
BPE for language modeling.

39
00:02:33,017 --> 00:02:38,857
It starts from the 256 possible byte
values and learns larger pieces.

40
00:02:38,897 --> 00:02:44,303
This way it can encode any UTF-8 text
without an unknown-character token.

41
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:48,104
How does byte pair encoding
create new tokens?

42
00:02:48,433 --> 00:02:53,328
Let's train a tiny tokenizer on
this text: hug, hug, hug, pug, pug.

43
00:02:53,667 --> 00:02:58,492
First, we split every word into single
characters. Each block is one token.

44
00:02:58,833 --> 00:03:02,123
In this example, we join
pieces of the same word,

45
00:03:02,163 --> 00:03:04,666
but never across the spaces between words.

46
00:03:05,333 --> 00:03:10,378
The vocabulary starts with just
four characters: h, u, g and p.

47
00:03:10,418 --> 00:03:12,653
They get the IDs 1 to 4.

48
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,574
Now we count every pair
of neighbouring tokens.

49
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:18,024
h followed by u appears three times.

50
00:03:18,267 --> 00:03:19,979
p followed by u, twice.

51
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:22,565
And u followed by g, five times.

52
00:03:22,833 --> 00:03:27,713
That is the most frequent pair, so we
merge it into a single new token: ug.

53
00:03:27,753 --> 00:03:29,735
It gets the next ID, 5.

54
00:03:30,067 --> 00:03:36,078
Then we repeat. h followed by ug appears
three times. p followed by ug, only twice.

55
00:03:36,367 --> 00:03:39,344
So h and ug merge into hug, with ID 6.

56
00:03:39,633 --> 00:03:44,528
One more round. The only pair left
is p and ug, with two occurrences.

57
00:03:44,568 --> 00:03:46,861
It becomes pug, with ID 7.

58
00:03:47,233 --> 00:03:52,548
Three merges later, the vocabulary has
grown from four characters to seven tokens,

59
00:03:52,588 --> 00:03:55,541
and every word in our
text is a single token.

60
00:03:55,867 --> 00:03:58,677
That is the core idea
of byte pair encoding:

61
00:03:58,717 --> 00:04:01,899
frequent patterns get
compact representations.

62
00:04:03,363 --> 00:04:06,443
The merges depend entirely
on the training data.

63
00:04:06,483 --> 00:04:09,093
So how does that data shape the tokenizer?

64
00:04:09,430 --> 00:04:13,885
First, the tokenizer and the language
model can use different datasets,

65
00:04:13,925 --> 00:04:17,693
and tokenizer training typically
requires much less text.

66
00:04:18,030 --> 00:04:21,635
Second, frequent patterns
get compact representations.

67
00:04:21,675 --> 00:04:25,223
The training mix shapes which
pieces enter the vocabulary.

68
00:04:25,563 --> 00:04:27,893
Third, language balance matters.

69
00:04:27,933 --> 00:04:33,169
English-heavy data can favour compact English
representations over other languages.

70
00:04:33,497 --> 00:04:35,662
Code and formatting matter too.

71
00:04:35,702 --> 00:04:39,282
The training mix influences
how whitespace is encoded,

72
00:04:39,322 --> 00:04:44,127
and it can favour formats such as YAML
over JSON, or the other way around.

73
00:04:44,463 --> 00:04:48,068
Finally, vocabulary choices
have lasting effects.

74
00:04:48,108 --> 00:04:52,693
The vocabulary usually stays fixed
while the language model is trained.

75
00:04:52,733 --> 00:04:56,208
And fewer tokens leave more
room in the context window.

76
00:04:57,397 --> 00:05:00,757
Let's look at a real tokenizer,
starting with numbers.

77
00:05:00,797 --> 00:05:06,672
This is Tiktokenizer, a web tool that
shows how OpenAI models split text.

78
00:05:06,712 --> 00:05:08,938
Every coloured block is one token.

79
00:05:09,263 --> 00:05:13,043
Take the sum 1234 plus 5678.

80
00:05:13,083 --> 00:05:20,353
With the GPT-4 tokenizer, 1234
becomes two tokens: 123 and 4.

81
00:05:20,393 --> 00:05:24,061
And 5678 becomes 567 and 8.

82
00:05:24,397 --> 00:05:31,012
Now a longer sum. 112345
splits into 112 and 345.

83
00:05:31,052 --> 00:05:36,458
The digits 1, 2, 3 are still there, but
they no longer form a token of their own.

84
00:05:36,797 --> 00:05:41,182
So the same digits end up in different
tokens, depending on their neighbours.

85
00:05:41,222 --> 00:05:44,080
Numbers split into tokens
of variable length.

86
00:05:45,263 --> 00:05:49,923
Here is the same effect at scale, for
the tokenizer of GPT-2 and GPT-3.

87
00:05:49,963 --> 00:05:54,268
The figure comes from a blog
post by Beren Millidge,

88
00:05:54,308 --> 00:05:59,396
and it covers the integers from 0 to 9999.

89
00:05:59,730 --> 00:06:01,590
Each cell is one integer.

90
00:06:01,630 --> 00:06:04,590
Moving one cell to the
right advances by one,

91
00:06:04,630 --> 00:06:07,534
and moving one row down
advances by one hundred.

92
00:06:07,863 --> 00:06:10,043
The colour shows how a number is split.

93
00:06:10,083 --> 00:06:13,264
Yellow means that the
entire number is one token.

94
00:06:13,597 --> 00:06:19,580
Red means one digit, then three
digits: 2249 becomes 2 and 249.

95
00:06:19,897 --> 00:06:26,071
Green means two digits, then two
digits: 2250 becomes 22 and 50.

96
00:06:26,397 --> 00:06:32,441
And blue means three digits, then
one digit: 2251 becomes 225 and 1.

97
00:06:32,763 --> 00:06:35,693
Three neighbouring numbers,
three different splits.

98
00:06:35,733 --> 00:06:39,973
The blog post is titled "Integer
tokenization is insane",

99
00:06:40,013 --> 00:06:41,927
and it is worth reading in full.

100
00:06:43,097 --> 00:06:48,027
This brings us back to letter counting,
sometimes called LLM dyslexia.

101
00:06:48,067 --> 00:06:51,161
Ask a model how many r
letters strawberry has.

102
00:06:51,497 --> 00:06:53,249
At the start of a line,

103
00:06:53,289 --> 00:06:59,484
the GPT-4 tokenizer splits strawberry
into three tokens: str, aw and berry.

104
00:06:59,830 --> 00:07:03,268
With a space in front, the
same word is one single token.

105
00:07:03,597 --> 00:07:07,871
Same word, different IDs. The
model sees token IDs, not letters.

106
00:07:08,197 --> 00:07:12,637
For such a question, an agentic LLM can
write and run a short piece of code

107
00:07:12,677 --> 00:07:16,378
that splits the word into
letters and counts them.

108
00:07:16,697 --> 00:07:22,487
It is important to develop an
intuition about when to ask the
model to do things via code,

109
00:07:22,527 --> 00:07:25,977
and when to trust its outputs
without additional tools.

110
00:07:27,163 --> 00:07:29,863
Not all languages are tokenized equally.

111
00:07:29,903 --> 00:07:33,480
Compare two greetings
with the GPT-4 tokenizer.

112
00:07:33,830 --> 00:07:36,561
The English greeting,
hello, is a single token.

113
00:07:36,897 --> 00:07:40,016
The Romanian greeting,
bună ziua, requires five.

114
00:07:40,363 --> 00:07:42,648
English dominates online text,

115
00:07:42,688 --> 00:07:46,588
so both the language model and
its tokenizer see more of it,

116
00:07:46,628 --> 00:07:49,830
and English can end up being
tokenized more compactly.

117
00:07:50,163 --> 00:07:54,443
This matters because the context
window is measured in tokens.

118
00:07:54,483 --> 00:07:57,276
Reasoning in a language
that requires more tokens

119
00:07:57,316 --> 00:08:01,461
for equivalent content can fill
the context window more quickly.

120
00:08:02,630 --> 00:08:07,419
Source code contains a lot of whitespace,
so let's tokenize two lines of Python.

121
00:08:07,763 --> 00:08:12,543
With the GPT-2 tokenizer,
the snippet takes 24 tokens.

122
00:08:12,583 --> 00:08:16,548
Look at the indentation: each
space is encoded separately,

123
00:08:16,588 --> 00:08:19,206
which results in longer token sequences.

124
00:08:19,530 --> 00:08:24,740
The GPT-4 tokenizer needs only
19 tokens for the same code.

125
00:08:24,780 --> 00:08:29,594
Here, several spaces are merged
together and treated as a single token.

126
00:08:29,930 --> 00:08:35,393
Fewer tokens for the same code
means that more code fits
within the same token budget.

127
00:08:36,563 --> 00:08:40,373
Before text reaches the tokenizer,
it has to become bytes.

128
00:08:40,413 --> 00:08:43,669
A very common way to do that is UTF-8.

129
00:08:43,997 --> 00:08:46,487
Every character has a Unicode code point,

130
00:08:46,527 --> 00:08:50,732
and UTF-8 converts that code
point into one to four bytes.

131
00:08:51,063 --> 00:08:53,558
The first bits of each
byte reveal its role.

132
00:08:53,598 --> 00:08:57,443
A byte starting with 0 is a
complete character on its own.

133
00:08:57,483 --> 00:09:05,953
The prefixes 110, 1110 and 11110 start
a sequence of two, three or four bytes.

134
00:09:05,993 --> 00:09:09,038
And every continuation
byte starts with 10.

135
00:09:09,363 --> 00:09:14,963
There are other encoding
schemes, like UTF-16 and UTF-32.

136
00:09:15,003 --> 00:09:19,753
UTF-32 is fixed length, whereas
UTF-8 is variable length,

137
00:09:19,793 --> 00:09:22,945
which can result in
shorter byte sequences.

138
00:09:23,263 --> 00:09:29,008
One of the major advantages of UTF-8 is
that it is backwards compatible with ASCII,

139
00:09:29,048 --> 00:09:31,531
which is a much simpler encoding of text.

140
00:09:32,730 --> 00:09:37,240
Because of this structure, not all
byte sequences are valid UTF-8.

141
00:09:37,280 --> 00:09:40,218
Take the character é.

142
00:09:40,563 --> 00:09:45,988
Its code point is U+00E9, and
it is encoded as two bytes,

143
00:09:46,028 --> 00:09:50,865
with the decimal values 195 and 169.

144
00:09:51,197 --> 00:09:56,892
The first byte begins with 110, which
indicates the start of a two-byte sequence.

145
00:09:56,932 --> 00:10:02,001
The second begins with 10, which
identifies it as a continuation byte.

146
00:10:02,330 --> 00:10:10,425
By contrast, the byte 128 cannot be decoded
on its own. In binary it is 10000000.

147
00:10:10,465 --> 00:10:13,960
Its 10 prefix marks it
as a continuation byte,

148
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:16,487
so a preceding start byte is required.

149
00:10:16,830 --> 00:10:22,875
This matters for tokenizers, because a
UTF-8 character may span several tokens.

150
00:10:22,915 --> 00:10:27,195
That is why the decode function
joins the bytes of all tokens first,

151
00:10:27,235 --> 00:10:29,425
and only then decodes them into text.

152
00:10:29,763 --> 00:10:32,298
When decoding with errors="replace",

153
00:10:32,338 --> 00:10:38,203
invalid UTF-8 sequences are replaced
with the Unicode replacement character.

154
00:10:38,243 --> 00:10:39,915
So when you see lots of these symbols,

155
00:10:39,955 --> 00:10:44,137
the post-processing pipeline could
not decode the bytes correctly.

156
00:10:45,330 --> 00:10:47,625
Some merges should never take place.

157
00:10:47,665 --> 00:10:52,490
For example, we might want to keep a word
separate from the punctuation after it.

158
00:10:52,530 --> 00:10:57,470
Regular expressions let us specify which
parts of the text should stay separate.

159
00:10:57,510 --> 00:11:00,543
Here is the one from the GPT-2 encoder.

160
00:11:00,930 --> 00:11:05,610
The regex splits text into smaller
chunks before BPE is applied.

161
00:11:05,650 --> 00:11:09,370
It recognizes English
contractions, groups of letters,

162
00:11:09,410 --> 00:11:13,434
groups of numbers, punctuation
and symbols, and whitespace.

163
00:11:13,763 --> 00:11:18,433
For example, the sentence "I'm buying
12 apples!" becomes six chunks.

164
00:11:18,473 --> 00:11:22,131
Notice that the space stays attached
to the word that follows it.

165
00:11:22,463 --> 00:11:28,083
BPE then processes each chunk separately,
and cannot merge across these boundaries.

166
00:11:29,263 --> 00:11:33,278
The GPT-4 tokenizer uses
a new regular expression.

167
00:11:33,318 --> 00:11:37,845
Its encoding is called cl100k_base,

168
00:11:37,885 --> 00:11:45,740
and it is the tokenizer used by the original
GPT-4, GPT-4 Turbo and GPT-3.5 Turbo.

169
00:11:46,063 --> 00:11:50,673
Let's run both patterns on the same
example text: underscore hello,

170
00:11:50,713 --> 00:11:56,788
I'M with a capital M, reading 12345,
an exclamation mark and a line break.

171
00:11:57,130 --> 00:11:59,945
The GPT-2 pattern gives nine chunks.

172
00:11:59,985 --> 00:12:04,135
The new pattern gives eight, and each
difference shows a rule that changed.

173
00:12:04,463 --> 00:12:10,588
English contractions are now case-insensitive,
so both 'm and 'M are recognized.

174
00:12:10,930 --> 00:12:18,540
Numbers are split into groups of one to
three digits: 12345 becomes 123 and 45.

175
00:12:18,580 --> 00:12:21,059
And the space before a
number is separated.

176
00:12:21,397 --> 00:12:26,142
Words can include a preceding symbol,
so underscore hello can stay together.

177
00:12:26,182 --> 00:12:30,395
Previously, only a preceding space
could attach to a group of letters.

178
00:12:30,730 --> 00:12:33,850
And punctuation can include
following line breaks,

179
00:12:33,890 --> 00:12:37,245
so the exclamation mark and
the line break stay together,

180
00:12:37,285 --> 00:12:39,820
whereas GPT-2 separates them.

181
00:12:40,997 --> 00:12:45,817
Besides ordinary tokens, a
tokenizer also has special tokens.

182
00:12:45,857 --> 00:12:49,584
The GPT-4 tokenizer defines five of them.

183
00:12:49,930 --> 00:12:53,490
End of text marks the end of
a document or text sequence.

184
00:12:53,830 --> 00:12:56,120
Three tokens support
filling in the middle.

185
00:12:56,160 --> 00:13:00,574
Imagine the sentence "The cat sat on
the mat", with the word sat missing.

186
00:13:00,897 --> 00:13:04,637
FIM prefix says: here is
the text before the gap.

187
00:13:04,677 --> 00:13:08,522
FIM suffix says: here is
the text after the gap.

188
00:13:08,562 --> 00:13:12,341
And FIM middle says: now
generate the missing text.

189
00:13:12,663 --> 00:13:15,678
So the model receives the
prefix token, The cat,

190
00:13:15,718 --> 00:13:18,983
the suffix token, on the
mat, and the middle token.

191
00:13:19,023 --> 00:13:21,906
Then it generates the missing middle: sat.

192
00:13:22,230 --> 00:13:26,665
The fifth token, end of prompt, marks
the end of the supplied prompt,

193
00:13:26,705 --> 00:13:29,433
after which the model
generates its completion.

194
00:13:29,763 --> 00:13:34,592
The fill-in-the-middle approach is described
in a paper by Bavarian and colleagues.

195
00:13:35,763 --> 00:13:38,518
How does the tokenizer treat
these special strings?

196
00:13:38,558 --> 00:13:43,231
Here, each special token is written
next to the same word as plain text.

197
00:13:43,563 --> 00:13:47,563
Note how each special token is a
single block with a single ID,

198
00:13:47,603 --> 00:13:51,321
while the plain word next to it
is split into ordinary pieces.

199
00:13:51,663 --> 00:13:56,848
Special tokens are indivisible markers
that structure messages and tool calls,

200
00:13:56,888 --> 00:13:59,928
for example a message
marker and a call marker.

201
00:13:59,968 --> 00:14:02,414
These two names are only illustrative.

202
00:14:02,763 --> 00:14:07,179
Tool names, arguments and content
typically use ordinary tokens.

203
00:14:07,497 --> 00:14:09,857
The model learns the
format during training,

204
00:14:09,897 --> 00:14:12,958
while external software
executes the tool calls.

205
00:14:14,130 --> 00:14:17,240
Special tokens also
structure conversations.

206
00:14:17,280 --> 00:14:21,515
Here is a short chat as the
GPT-4 tokenizer sees it.

207
00:14:21,863 --> 00:14:24,473
‹|im_start|› marks the
beginning of a message.

208
00:14:24,513 --> 00:14:27,878
‹|im_sep|› separates the message
header from its content.

209
00:14:27,918 --> 00:14:30,607
And ‹|im_end|› marks the end of a message.

210
00:14:30,930 --> 00:14:36,170
Role labels such as system, user and
assistant identify who is speaking.

211
00:14:36,210 --> 00:14:40,673
In this example, these labels are
ordinary text enclosed by special tokens,

212
00:14:40,713 --> 00:14:46,121
so the model learns to interpret them as
roles when they appear in that position.

213
00:14:46,463 --> 00:14:49,428
The sequence ends with an
open assistant header.

214
00:14:49,468 --> 00:14:52,617
From this point, the model
generates its reply.

215
00:14:53,797 --> 00:14:56,863
To work with these tokenizers in Python,

216
00:14:56,903 --> 00:15:01,847
you can use tiktoken, a fast BPE
tokenizer for OpenAI's models.

217
00:15:01,887 --> 00:15:05,055
It is installed with pip install tiktoken.

218
00:15:05,397 --> 00:15:07,152
You load an encoding by name,

219
00:15:07,192 --> 00:15:11,092
or you ask for the encoding that
belongs to a specific model.

220
00:15:11,132 --> 00:15:16,063
Then encode turns text into token
IDs, and decode turns them back.

221
00:15:16,397 --> 00:15:18,337
According to its documentation,

222
00:15:18,377 --> 00:15:24,322
tiktoken is between three and
six times faster than a
comparable open source tokenizer.

223
00:15:25,497 --> 00:15:27,777
You can also extend tiktoken.

224
00:15:27,817 --> 00:15:31,647
You create your own Encoding
object exactly the way you want,

225
00:15:31,687 --> 00:15:33,266
and simply pass it around.

226
00:15:33,597 --> 00:15:35,832
In this example from the documentation,

227
00:15:35,872 --> 00:15:44,259
a new encoding called cl100k_im reuses the
pattern and the merges of cl100k_base.

228
00:15:44,597 --> 00:15:48,842
It then adds two special tokens,
‹|im_start|› and ‹|im_end|›.

229
00:15:48,882 --> 00:15:53,113
So you can add more special tokens
afterwards, each with a new ID.

230
00:15:54,297 --> 00:15:57,812
A new token in the tokenizer
is only the first step.

231
00:15:57,852 --> 00:16:00,776
Adding a token to a
pretrained model takes four.

232
00:16:01,097 --> 00:16:03,540
One: add the new token to the tokenizer.

233
00:16:03,863 --> 00:16:09,123
Two: expand the input embedding matrix
and the output vocabulary projection.

234
00:16:09,163 --> 00:16:13,693
Each one grows from V × d to (V+1) × d,

235
00:16:13,733 --> 00:16:18,024
where V is the vocabulary size
and d is the vector size.

236
00:16:18,363 --> 00:16:22,603
The new row of the embedding matrix
is a vector for the new token.

237
00:16:22,643 --> 00:16:27,743
The new row of the output projection holds
the weights for predicting the new token,

238
00:16:27,783 --> 00:16:30,588
so there is one more possible next token.

239
00:16:30,628 --> 00:16:34,645
The transformer blocks in between
keep the same matrix sizes.

240
00:16:34,963 --> 00:16:38,656
Three: initialize the new
parameters, that is, the new rows.

241
00:16:38,997 --> 00:16:42,372
Four: fine-tune on examples
containing the new token.

242
00:16:42,697 --> 00:16:46,787
Adding special tokens can support
adapting a base model for chat,

243
00:16:46,827 --> 00:16:51,037
or introducing tool-use formats
in chat and coding models.

244
00:16:51,077 --> 00:16:55,475
Fine-tuning teaches the model to
interpret and generate these formats.

245
00:16:56,663 --> 00:16:59,273
The second library to
know is SentencePiece.

246
00:16:59,597 --> 00:17:03,632
SentencePiece supports both
tokenizer training and inference,

247
00:17:03,672 --> 00:17:09,115
whereas tiktoken focuses primarily on
inference with an already trained tokenizer.

248
00:17:09,463 --> 00:17:11,969
It supports multiple algorithms,

249
00:17:12,009 --> 00:17:16,703
including BPE and Unigram,
whereas tiktoken focuses on BPE.

250
00:17:17,030 --> 00:17:23,730
SentencePiece is used by models such as
Llama 2, Mistral 7B and Mixtral 8x7B.

251
00:17:23,770 --> 00:17:28,716
Llama 3 moved from SentencePiece
to a tiktoken-based tokenizer.

252
00:17:29,897 --> 00:17:35,428
What is in a SentencePiece BPE vocabulary?
It contains four types of tokens.

253
00:17:35,763 --> 00:17:39,773
Special tokens are reserved
tokens with predefined functions:

254
00:17:39,813 --> 00:17:41,145
‹unk› for unknown tokens,

255
00:17:41,185 --> 00:17:43,793
‹s› for the start of a sentence,

256
00:17:43,833 --> 00:17:47,962
‹/s› for the end of a sentence,
and optionally ‹pad› for padding.

257
00:17:48,297 --> 00:17:50,997
Byte tokens represent individual bytes.

258
00:17:51,037 --> 00:17:55,912
They are used as a fallback for characters
not covered by the normal vocabulary,

259
00:17:55,952 --> 00:17:57,658
when byte_fallback is True.

260
00:17:57,997 --> 00:18:00,457
Merge tokens are subword tokens,

261
00:18:00,497 --> 00:18:06,472
learned by repeatedly merging
frequently occurring neighbouring
symbols during BPE training.

262
00:18:06,797 --> 00:18:11,297
And single-character tokens represent
individual Unicode characters,

263
00:18:11,337 --> 00:18:17,742
retained according to the specified
character_coverage, for example 0.995.

264
00:18:17,782 --> 00:18:21,292
If a codepoint is too rare to
fall within that coverage,

265
00:18:21,332 --> 00:18:23,521
it is not added to the vocabulary.

266
00:18:24,697 --> 00:18:30,146
Whitespace needs care. Look at these
two sentences in the GPT-4 tokenizer.

267
00:18:30,463 --> 00:18:33,403
At the start of the
text, dog is one token.

268
00:18:33,443 --> 00:18:36,763
After the word "the", it
appears with a preceding space,

269
00:18:36,803 --> 00:18:39,447
and that is a different
token with a different ID.

270
00:18:39,797 --> 00:18:43,861
SentencePiece represents whitespace
explicitly, with a marker symbol.

271
00:18:44,197 --> 00:18:46,187
If add_dummy_prefix is True,

272
00:18:46,227 --> 00:18:49,867
a whitespace marker is also
added at the start of the input.

273
00:18:49,907 --> 00:18:53,910
This treats the first word like
a word preceded by a space.

274
00:18:54,230 --> 00:18:58,340
That reduces the need to learn separate
token variants for the same word,

275
00:18:58,380 --> 00:19:00,395
with and without a preceding space.

276
00:19:01,563 --> 00:19:04,632
Let's put tiktoken and
SentencePiece side by side.

277
00:19:04,963 --> 00:19:08,973
Basic representation: tiktoken
works on UTF-8 bytes,

278
00:19:09,013 --> 00:19:12,388
SentencePiece on Unicode text and pieces.

279
00:19:12,730 --> 00:19:16,640
Tokenization methods: tiktoken uses BPE.

280
00:19:16,680 --> 00:19:22,886
SentencePiece offers BPE, Unigram,
character-level and word-level tokenization.

281
00:19:23,230 --> 00:19:27,310
Pre-tokenization: tiktoken
applies regex constraints.

282
00:19:27,350 --> 00:19:31,358
SentencePiece uses its own
normalization and whitespace scheme.

283
00:19:31,697 --> 00:19:35,602
Training: tiktoken primarily
provides prebuilt encodings,

284
00:19:35,642 --> 00:19:38,362
although an educational trainer exists.

285
00:19:38,402 --> 00:19:42,186
SentencePiece is a full
tokenizer-training toolkit.

286
00:19:42,530 --> 00:19:47,135
Whitespace: in tiktoken it
depends on the regex and on BPE.

287
00:19:47,175 --> 00:19:50,399
SentencePiece uses an
explicit whitespace symbol.

288
00:19:50,730 --> 00:19:55,475
And unknown characters: tiktoken
has a byte-based representation.

289
00:19:55,515 --> 00:20:00,027
SentencePiece uses the ‹unk› token,
or an optional byte fallback.

290
00:20:01,197 --> 00:20:03,045
Let's close with five takeaways.

291
00:20:03,363 --> 00:20:05,893
Tokens are the model's units of text.

292
00:20:05,933 --> 00:20:10,254
The tokenizer converts text into
a sequence of numerical IDs.

293
00:20:10,597 --> 00:20:14,382
BPE builds tokens by
merging frequent pairs.

294
00:20:14,422 --> 00:20:18,590
Its training data shapes which
patterns get compact representations.

295
00:20:18,930 --> 00:20:22,285
Fewer tokens mean more room
in the context window.

296
00:20:22,325 --> 00:20:25,784
Different languages and formats
use that space differently.

297
00:20:26,130 --> 00:20:29,690
The tokenizer can split
words in unexpected places.

298
00:20:29,730 --> 00:20:33,910
Splits don't always follow
syllables or familiar word parts.

299
00:20:33,950 --> 00:20:37,275
A token can also include
the space before a word,

300
00:20:37,315 --> 00:20:42,929
so "hello" and " hello" may use different
tokens, depending on the tokenizer.

301
00:20:43,263 --> 00:20:45,613
And special tokens provide structure.

302
00:20:45,653 --> 00:20:48,243
The model learns the
format during training,

303
00:20:48,283 --> 00:20:51,236
while external software
executes tool calls.

304
00:20:52,430 --> 00:20:55,955
All sources are listed on
the last slide of the deck:

305
00:20:55,995 --> 00:20:58,140
the papers, the code and libraries,

306
00:20:58,180 --> 00:21:04,001
the tools and articles, and Andrej Karpathy's
video "Let's build the GPT Tokenizer".

307
00:21:04,330 --> 00:21:09,635
To explore further, open Tiktokenizer and
try your own text. Thanks for watching.

